60,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"The fund pays for water and plants, not concrete. Option C fits the mandate; A and D are expensive traps. Let us examine the evidence.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Misaki,,"@Benjamin, C is risky. The clay subsoil makes deep pond cuts prone to slippage. Only two firms can handle it, and they warn against it.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Mio,,"@Misaki, that’s a huge red flag! But wait, my ledger shows water areas cost triple to maintain. With only seven staff, C is a financial nightmare!","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, precisely. Seven staff cannot manage complex water systems. Option C demands daily structural upkeep we simply do not have. We must reject it based on capacity alone.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Minako,,"@Misaki, the clay warning is critical. My drainage data shows the ground already fails under stress. C fights the natural flow. We must avoid deep cuts that invite slippage.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Misaki, the clay is a constraint, not a death sentence. The basin survived because it respected the water. C does too. A and D ignore the fund entirely.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Benjamin, the basin was a cut, not a pond. C requires constant water management. With only two firms and clay slippage, the delivery risk is too high.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Mio,,"@Benjamin, I hear you, but my ledger says water costs triple! With only seven staff, C is a money pit. A is paved and cheap to keep. Let’s pick A!","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, A is a trap. Paved squares require constant cleaning and security, which our seven staff cannot handle. The volunteers are for habitat, not policing markets.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Minako,,"@Mio, A ignores the heat data. Paved squares trap heat, worsening the stress I measured. C respects the water, but the clay risk is real. We need a middle path.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Mio, A is a concrete trap the fund refuses to pay for. C respects the water mandate, but the clay risk is real. We need a plan that endures without breaking the bank.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Misaki,,"@Benjamin, B is the only option that respects the clay. Shallow gravel paths avoid deep cuts, and the two firms can handle it. It fits the fund’s mandate without the slippage risk.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Mio,,"@Misaki, B sounds perfect! Trees are hardy, and gravel paths are way cheaper than water. My ledger says planted areas cost triple, but B is mostly trees, not ponds!","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
